==========================================

The real inspiration here is that PtokaX's deflood is really doing what a generic
firewall should do. Really, building too much into the hub limits it's ability to
adapt. So, here are some options.

Ports
-----

	411	TCPPorts, bound by PtokaX
	5411	the NMDCS port, bound by the TLS terminator

The loopback address in TLSProxyAddress needs no rule. Nothing off the host can reach
it.

SYN floods
----------

isFlooder never sees a SYN flood. The kernel completes the handshake before accept()
returns, so a half-open flood is exhausted before the hub is involved.

	net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies = 1

nftables adds synproxy on top of that. pf has synproxy state. The iptables and the
frontend files below have neither.

What each file covers
---------------------

	nftables.nft		rate limit, plus synproxy
	iptables.rules		rate limit through ipset, no synproxy
	pf.conf			rate limit through max-src-conn-rate, plus synproxy state
	firewalld.md		rate limit through rich rules
	ufw.md			partial, see the file

An IDS reads nothing on the NMDCS port. The traffic is encrypted between the client
and the terminator, so signature rules apply to 411 only.
